  "findings": [
    {
      "area": "release gate",
      "conclusion": "verify_release_profile requires a dict, a string optimized Cargo opt_level in 1/2/3/s/z, and actual bool false for debug_assertions and test. Missing fields, numeric levels and integer false-like values do not pass. It makes no claim about a particular optimization level, architecture, performance, or hermetic compiler behavior."
    },
    {
      "area": "default product builder",
      "conclusion": "build gains release=False and the CLI adds an explicit store_true --release flag. Without that flag, the command, feature choices, --locked, --no-default-features and dev labeling retain their prior behavior. With it, --release is present and actual Cargo profile validation occurs before executable copying and success-receipt creation."
    },
    {
      "area": "successful and failed receipts",
      "conclusion": "The product builder still creates a new private output/target, preserves raw Cargo stdout/stderr, returns a failing process code without creating build-receipt.json, compares committed source and toolchain before/after, verifies actual artifact package/feature/source paths and copies a byte-bound executable. The new release check does not convert failed or unoptimized builds into success."
    },
    {
      "area": "candidate consumer",
      "conclusion": "validate_build requires recorded actual_cargo_profile to equal the artifact profile for release, then checks optimization and still compares the complete artifact to retained Cargo output. The prior raw log successful-finish check, expected source, binary hash, source manifest, default features, command/profile correspondence, and toolchain requirements remain."
    },
    {
      "area": "runner builder",
      "conclusion": "build_runner retains its existing explicit release flag and now validates the parsed Cargo profile before copying a successful runner. Its source/toolchain before-and-after, target-bound executable and recorded target reuse state remain. Existing target reuse is recorded, not relabeled as an absent private target."
    },
    {
      "area": "execution certification boundary",
      "conclusion": "The original run_candidate, original deterministic scorer bindings, raw gate/exit agreement, replay equality, drift controls and release_certified=false scope are byte-identical. The delta neither rewrites fixed F/D receipts nor alters repository source locks or default benchmark acceptance."
    },
    {
      "area": "meaning of the tests",
      "conclusion": "The four new controls are command/profile and receipt-validation unit controls. Their failed fake build remains exit17 with no successful receipt. Their mocked successful validation is not a real Cargo build witness. The eight unchanged controls additionally exercise actual temporary Git/file identities, raw log mismatch rejection, real subprocess nonzero exit preservation, existing-output rejection and disposable drift copies."
    }
  ],
  "independent_reviewers": [
    "/root/build_validation"
  ],
  "limits": [
    "These 12 unit controls and the static review do not certify that the new release option has built a real optimized product on this source. Any actual build or runtime result must carry its own immutable source, raw Cargo artifact, profile and binary receipt.",
    "The review covers only the listed four original PR153 paths. The scanner repair authored by this reviewer is excluded and has a separate non-author review from /root/pr_audit.",
    "Final remotely published union P coverage/MCP/Clippy and source-integrity gates are still separate; previous P615 failures are not overwritten."
  ],
